`x` (in the order that the\n7162 |     data are stored in memory). If `x` is masked, the new array will be\n7163 |     masked, and the new mask will be a repetition of the old one.\n7164 | \n7165 |     See Also\n7166 |     --------\n7167 |     numpy.resize : Equivalent function in the top level NumPy module.\n7168 | \n7169 |     Examples\n7170 |     --------\n7171 |     >>> import numpy.ma as ma\n7172 |     >>> a = ma.array([[1, 2] ,[3, 4]])\n7173 |     >>> a[0, 1] = ma.masked\n7174 |     >>> a\n7175 |     masked_array(\n7176 |       data=[[1, --],\n7177 |             [3, 4]],\n7178 |       mask=[[False,  True],\n7179 |             [False, False]],\n7180 |       fill_value=999999)\n7181 |     >>> np.resize(a, (3, 3))\n7182 |     masked_array(\n7183 |       data=[[1, 2, 3],\n7184 |             [4, 1, 2],\n7185 |             [3, 4, 1]],\n7186 |       mask=False,\n7187 |       fill_value=999999)\n7188 |     >>> ma.resize(a, (3, 3))\n7189 |     masked_array(\n7190 |       data=[[1, --, 3],\n7191 |             [4, 1, --],\n7192 |             [3, 4, 1]],\n7193 |       mask=[[False,  True, False],\n7194 |             [False, False,  True],\n7195 |             [False, False, False]],\n7196 |       fill_value=999999)\n7197 | \n7198 |     A MaskedArray is always returned, regardless of the input type.\n7199 | \n7200 |     >>> a = np.array([[1, 2] ,[3, 4]])\n7201 |     >>> ma.resize(a, (3, 3))\n7202 |     masked_array(\n7203 |       data=[[1, 2, 3],\n7204 |             [4, 1, 2],\n7205 |             [3, 4, 1]],\n7206 |       mask=False,\n7207 |       fill_value=999999)\n7208 | \n7209 |     \"\"\"\n7210 |     # We can't use _frommethods here, as N.resize is notoriously whiny.\n7211 |     m = getmask(x)\n7212 |     if m is not nomask:\n7213 |         m = np.resize(m, new_shape)\n7214 |     result = np.resize(x, new_shape).view(get_masked_subclass(x))\n7215 |     if result.ndim:\n7216 |         result._mask = m\n7217 |     return result\n```\n\nFound function `numpy/ma/core.py:si