 expr : Expression\n2884 |                The expression whose series is to be expanded.\n2885 | \n2886 |         x : Symbol\n2887 |             It is the variable of the expression to be calculated.\n2888 | \n2889 |         x0 : Value\n2890 |              The value around which ``x`` is calculated. Can be any value\n2891 |              from ``-oo`` to ``oo``.\n2892 | \n2893 |         n : Value\n2894 |             The value used to represent the order in terms of ``x**n``,\n2895 |             up to which the series is to be expanded.\n2896 | \n2897 |         dir : String, optional\n2898 |               The series-expansion can be bi-directional. If ``dir=\"+\"``,\n2899 |               then (x->x0+). If ``dir=\"-\", then (x->x0-). For infinite\n2900 |               ``x0`` (``oo`` or ``-oo``), the ``dir`` argument is determined\n2901 |               from the direction of the infinity (i.e., ``dir=\"-\"`` for\n2902 |               ``oo``).\n2903 | \n2904 |         logx : optional\n2905 |                It is used to replace any log(x) in the returned series with a\n2906 |                symbolic value rather than evaluating the actual value.\n2907 | \n2908 |         cdir : optional\n2909 |                It stands for complex direction, and indicates the direction\n2910 |                from which the expansion needs to be evaluated.\n2911 | \n2912 |         Examples\n2913 |         ========\n2914 | \n2915 |         >>> from sympy import cos, exp, tan\n2916 |         >>> from sympy.abc import x, y\n2917 |         >>> cos(x).series()\n2918 |         1 - x**2/2 + x**4/24 + O(x**6)\n2919 |         >>> cos(x).series(n=4)\n2920 |         1 - x**2/2 + O(x**4)\n2921 |         >>> cos(x).series(x, x0=1, n=2)\n2922 |         cos(1) - (x - 1)*sin(1) + O((x - 1)**2, (x, 1))\n2923 |         >>> e = cos(x + exp(y))\n2924 |         >>> e.series(y, n=2)\n2925 |         cos(x + 1) - y*sin(x + 1) + O(y**2)\n2926 |         >>> e.series(x, n=2)\n2927 |         cos(exp(y)) - x*sin(exp(y)) + O(x**2)\n2928 | \n2929 |         If ``n=None`` then a generator of the series terms will be returned.\n2930 | \n2931 |         >>> term=cos(x).series(n=None)\n2932 |         >>> [next(term) for i in range(2)]\n2933 |         [1, -x**2/2]\n2934 | \n2935 |         For ``dir=+`` (default) the series is calculated from the right and\n2936 |         for ``dir=-`` the series from the left. For smooth functions this\n2937 |         flag will not alter the results.\n2938 | \n2939 |         >>> abs(x).series(dir=\"+\")\n2940 |         x\n2941 |         >>> abs(x).series(dir=\"-\")\n2942 |         -x\n2943 |         >>> f = tan(x)\n2944 |         >>> f.series(x, 2, 6, \"+\")\n2945 |         tan(2) + (1 + tan(2)**2)*(x - 2) + (x - 2)**2*(tan(2)**3 + tan(2)) +\n2946 |         (x - 2)**3*(1/3 + 4*tan(2)**2/3 + tan(2)**4) + (x - 2)**4*(tan(2)**5 +\n2947 |         5*tan(2)**3/3 + 2*tan(2)/3) + (x - 2)**5*(2/15 + 17*tan(2)**2/15 +\n2948 |         2*tan(2)**4 + tan(2)**6) + O((x - 2)**6, (x, 2))\n2949 | \n2950 |         >>> f.series(x, 2, 3, \"-\")\n2951 |         tan(2) + (2 - x)*(-tan(2)**2 - 1) + (2 - x)**2*(tan(2)**3 + tan(2))\n2952 |         + O((x - 2)**3, (x, 2))\n2953 | \n2954 |         For rational expressions this method may return original expression without the Order term.\n2955 |         >>> (1/x).series(x, n=8)\n2956 |         1/x\n2957 | \n2958 |         Returns\n2959 |         =======\n2960 | \n2961 |         Expr : Expression\n2962 |             Series expansion of the expression about x0\n2963 | \n2964 |         Raises\n2965 |         ======\n2966 | \n2967 |         TypeError\n2968 |             If \"n\" and \"x0\" are infinity objects\n2969 | \n2970 |         PoleError\n2971 |             If \"x0\" is an infinity object\n2972 | \n2973 |         \"\"\"\n2974 |         if x is None:\n2975 |             syms = self.free_symbols\n2976 |             if not syms:\n2977 |                 return self\n2978 |             elif len(syms) > 1:\n2979 |                 raise